{{#extend "layout" showSource=true}}

  {{#content "main"}}
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Example 15 &middot; partial transforms</p>
    <h1>Brew guide</h1>
    <p>
      The two ways we brew at home. Jump to the <a href="#recipe-v60">V60</a> or the
      <a href="#recipe-cafetiere">cafetiere</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="grid">
    {{> "recipes/v60"}}
    {{> "recipes/cafetiere"}}
  </div>
  {{/content}}

  {{#content "demo"}}
  <p>
    <strong>Each card is a short text file.</strong> <code>src/partials/recipes/v60.recipe</code>
    is a few <code>name: value</code> lines, a blank line, and one step per line. The
    <code>recipe</code> transform in <code>transforms.js</code> turns that into the card, and
    takes the card's id from the partial's name, so <code>recipes/v60</code> can be linked to
    as <code>#recipe-v60</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Unlike the price list, a recipe is compiled first.</strong> Its transform sets no
    <code>compile</code>, so the file is a template, as an <code>.html</code> partial is: the
    last step of each recipe links to the <a href="{{link 'prices'}}">price list</a> with
    <code>link</code>, and that link is checked like every other.
  </p>
  {{/content}}

{{/extend}}
